  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1057 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nFriday was a near rinse and repeat of Thursday with much of the \nMid-South struggling to reach the 80s thanks to cloud cover. \nClouds have mostly eroded out leaving a pleasant night with \ncurrent temperatures in the 60s to lower 70s. With pockets of \nclearing skies amidst a shallow low-level inversion, patchy fog is\nlikely across portions of the Mid-South, especially in low- lying\nareas. Any fog that does develop will quickly lift shortly after \nsunrise thanks to mixing.\n\nWe remain sandwiched between two upper-level systems, a ridge\ncurrently centered over the Texas Gulf coast and an upper-level\nlow centered over the North Carolina coast. This ridge will\ncontinue to broaden moving into Friday and Saturday leading to a\nslight warming trend, pushing temperatures just above normal with\nhighs in the 80s to lower 90s. Come late Saturday, this ridge\nlooks to get shoved a little further south and begins to break\ndown as the aforementioned upper low (nor'easter) pushes into the\nnortheast nudging us here in the Mid-South back into upper-level\nnorthwest flow. A few embedded shortwaves could give us enough\nlift for a few pop-up thunderstorms Sunday and into early week.\nThough appreciable rainfall to help alleviate ongoing drought\nconditions is not expected. Temperatures will continue to warm\ninto the upper 80s to lower 90s through early week.\n\nHeading into midweek, uncertainty increases with the overall\nweather pattern. Ensembles remain in decent agreement with an\nupper-level trough phasing with a surface low, but timing and\noverall strength of these systems will be greatly influenced by\nHurricane Polo in the Pacific. We could finally see some\nappreciable rainfall with this next system, but this will be\ngreatly dependent on Polo's influence. There is a pretty\nmentionable (15-20 degree) temperature spread beginning Wednesday\nthrough the end of the seven day period. ENS continues to favor a\ncooler solution with highs in the 70s and 80s whereas the GEFS\nsolution favors a warmer trend with highs in the upper 80s come\nWednesday. Bottom line, this remains a wait and see game, but one\nthing's for sure, we do not foresee record-breaking highs to\nround out our week. Details will be ironed out in future forecast\npackages as solutions become more in line.\n\nAEH",
